This is a very common Spring interview question:
“Why does @Transactional sometimes not work in Spring Framework / Spring Boot?”

The main reason is Spring uses Proxy-based AOP.

1️⃣ How @Transactional Works

When you write:

@Transactional
public void createUser() {
    // database operations
}

Spring does not modify the method directly.
Instead it creates a proxy around the bean.

Flow:

Client
 ↓
Proxy (transaction start)
 ↓
createUser()
 ↓
Proxy (commit / rollback)

So the transaction logic runs inside the proxy.

2️⃣ Problem: Internal Method Calls

Example:

@Service
public class UserService {

    public void methodA() {
        methodB();   // internal call
    }

    @Transactional
    public void methodB() {
        // database operation
    }
}

Client calls:

userService.methodA();

Flow:

Client
 ↓
Proxy
 ↓
methodA()
 ↓
methodB()   ❌ Direct call (proxy bypassed)

Because methodA and methodB are inside the same class, the call does not go through the proxy.

Result:

@Transactional will NOT work
3️⃣ Correct Way

Move transactional method to another service.

Example:

Service 1
@Service
public class UserService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderService orderService;

    public void methodA() {
        orderService.methodB();
    }
}
Service 2
@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Transactional
    public void methodB() {
        // database operation
    }
}

Now flow becomes:

Client
 ↓
UserService Proxy
 ↓
OrderService Proxy
 ↓
methodB() (transaction works)
4️⃣ Other Reasons @Transactional May Not Work
1️⃣ Method is private
@Transactional
private void saveUser()

Proxy cannot intercept private methods.

2️⃣ Method is final

CGLIB cannot override final methods.

3️⃣ Called inside constructor

Spring proxy is not fully initialized.

5️⃣ Real Interview Answer

@Transactional sometimes does not work because Spring uses proxy-based AOP. If a transactional method is called internally within the same class,
 the call bypasses the proxy, so transaction management is not applied.


 Great! Let’s break down Spring’s @Transactional propagation types with simple examples. This is very common in senior Java/Spring interviews.

 1️⃣ REQUIRED (Default)

 If a transaction exists, join it.

 If no transaction exists, create a new one.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRED)
 public void methodA() {
     // part of transaction
 }
 Example
 methodA (REQUIRED) → calls methodB (REQUIRED)

 Both run in same transaction.

 If methodB fails, everything rolls back.

 ✅ Most common and default.

 2️⃣ REQUIRES_NEW

 Always suspends current transaction and starts a new transaction.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW)
 public void methodB() { ... }
 Example
 methodA (REQUIRED) → calls methodB (REQUIRES_NEW)

 methodB has separate transaction.

 If methodB fails, only methodB rolls back, methodA continues.

 3️⃣ SUPPORTS

 If a transaction exists, join it.

 If no transaction exists, run without transaction.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.SUPPORTS)
 public void methodC() { ... }

 Useful for read-only operations.

 4️⃣ MANDATORY

 Must be called inside an existing transaction.

 Throws Exception if no transaction exists.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.MANDATORY)
 public void methodD() { ... }

 Ensures transaction is already active.

 5️⃣ NOT_SUPPORTED

 Do not run in a transaction.

 If a transaction exists, suspend it.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NOT_SUPPORTED)
 public void methodE() { ... }

 Useful for operations that should never be transactional, e.g., logging.

 6️⃣ NEVER

 Throws exception if a transaction exists.

 Runs only when no transaction is active.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NEVER)
 public void methodF() { ... }
 7️⃣ NESTED

 Creates a nested transaction using savepoints.

 If nested fails, rolls back only nested part, not parent transaction.

 @Transactional(propagation = Propagation.NESTED)
 public void methodG() { ... }

 Only supported by databases that support savepoints (like MySQL, PostgreSQL).

 🔑 Quick Reference Table
| Propagation   | Behavior                      |
| ------------- | ----------------------------- |
| REQUIRED      | Join existing or create new   |
| REQUIRES_NEW  | Always new, suspends existing |
| SUPPORTS      | Join if exists, else no tx    |
| MANDATORY     | Must exist, else exception    |
| NOT_SUPPORTED | Suspend existing, run non-tx  |
| NEVER         | Throw exception if tx exists  |
| NESTED        | Nested tx with savepoints     |

 🔑 Interview Tip

 Most common: REQUIRED and REQUIRES_NEW

 Trick question: internal method calls + propagation type

 Always mention: “Proxy-based AOP affects transactions”